Multiply by the conjugate $\sqrt{4x+9}+3$:
\[
\frac{(\sqrt{4x+9}-3)(\sqrt{4x+9}+3)}{x(\sqrt{4x+9}+3)}
=\frac{4x+9-9}{x(\sqrt{4x+9}+3)}
=\frac{4x}{x(\sqrt{4x+9}+3)}
=\frac{4}{\sqrt{4x+9}+3}.
\]
Now let $x\to0$: $\displaystyle \frac{4}{\sqrt{9}+3}= \frac{4}{3+3}= \frac{4}{6}= \frac{2}{3}.$

\[
\begin{aligned}
f'(2)&=\lim_{h\to0}\frac{(2+h)^{2}-2^{2}}{h}\\
&=\lim_{h\to0}\frac{4+4h+h^{2}-4}{h}\\
&=\lim_{h\to0}\frac{4h+h^{2}}{h}
=\lim_{h\to0}(4+h)=4.
\end{aligned}
\]

First derivative: $p'(x)=\frac{2x}{x^2+1}$.
Second derivative using quotient rule:
$p''(x)=\frac{2(x^2+1)-2x(2x)}{(x^2+1)^2}
=\frac{2x^2+2-4x^2}{(x^2+1)^2}
=\frac{2-2x^2}{(x^2+1)^2}
=\frac{2(1-x^2)}{(x^2+1)^2}$.
Set $p''(x)=0$: numerator $2(1-x^2)=0 \Rightarrow x=\pm1$.
Check sign change: for $|x|<1$, $1-x^2>0$ so $p''>0$ (concave up); for $|x|>1$, $1-x^2<0$ so $p''<0$ (concave down).
Thus inflection points at $x=-1$ and $x=1$, with coordinates $(-1,\ln 2)$ and $(1,\ln 2)$.
